## Comms — five verbs, subject line starts with the verb

```bash
python .claude/comms/comms.py read run-{NN} lane-{x} --unread
python .claude/comms/comms.py post run-{NN} lane-{x} --wo <row-id> --subject "STATUS <row>: <one line>" "<body>"
```

| Verb | When | Body must contain |
|---|---|---|
| `STATUS` | every row start and row close | row id, one line, current commit sha |
| `BLOCKED` | never speculatively | the evidence: command + output excerpt |
| `CROSS-REQ` | you need another lane's territory | target lane, exact path(s), why it cannot be avoided |
| `VERIFY` | a row reaches AWAITING_VERIFICATION | row id, commit sha, evidence paths under `evidence/{x}/` |
| `WATCH` | gate → you only; you never post it | the instruction. **A WATCH is obeyed, not debated.** |

`VERIFY-PASS` / `VERIFY-BOUNCE` are the gate's two replies on the `VERIFY` verb, not additional
verbs. No lane invents a sixth verb.

There is no `work-queue.md`, no `done.md`, no `findings.md`. Markdown files are artifacts you
write; they are never a coordination channel. The bus is the only mechanism.

## VERIFY-BOUNCE

If the gate bounces your row, it comes back with the failing evidence. Re-open that row
**before** starting a new one. Bounces are expected and healthy.

